 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# [Bug]: collections.abc objects are no longer subscriptable\n\n## Description\n\nAfter a recent update, I noticed that collections.abc objects that should be subscriptable (like MutableSet, Iterator, ByteString) are no longer working with subscript notation. This is breaking my code that relies on this functionality.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```python\nimport collections.abc\n\n# This should work but now fails\ncollections.abc.MutableSet[int]\n\n# This also fails\ncollections.abc.Iterator[int]\n\n# Class inheritance with subscriptable collections also fails\nclass Derived(collections.abc.Iterator[int]):\n    pass\n```\n\n## Actual outcome\n\nWhen trying to use subscript notation with collections.abc objects, I get a TypeError indicating that the object is not subscriptable.\n\n## Expected outcome\n\nCollections.abc objects should be subscriptable as they have been since Python 3.9. The code above should work without errors.\n\n## Reproduction script\n\n```python\nimport collections.abc\n\ntry:\n    result = collections.abc.MutableSet[int]\n    print(\"Success: MutableSet is subscriptable\")\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n\ntry:\n    class Derived(collections.abc.Iterator[int]):\n        pass\n    print(\"Success: Can inherit from subscripted Iterator\")\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n```\n\n## Additional information\n\nThis functionality was working in previous versions. It seems the `__class_getitem__` method is no longer available on these collection classes.\n\n## Environment\n\n- Python version: 3.10\n- OS: Ubuntu 20.04\n</issue>\n",
